use std
into mold_amg.amni
dialect js

This file is a library built for the web as a wasm module; its `main()` does nothing. JavaScript drives it through its exported functions (every name starting with am_ is exported). It is a port of the aggregation algebraic multigrid preconditioned conjugate gradient solver in cad/mold_amg.js (`diagOf`, `aggregate`, `galerkin`, `chol`, `amgSetup`, `gs`, `cyc`, `amgPcg`). Results are compared bit for bit with the JavaScript solver, so every floating-point formula keeps the JavaScript evaluation order exactly: each sum starts at its stated first value and adds or subtracts its terms one at a time in loop order, products and quotients group left to right as written, and nothing is fused, reordered or vectorized across terms. Reals are f64. Matrices are compressed sparse rows: row i holds entries k from rp[i] up to but not including rp[i+1], with column ci[k] and value va[k]. Level arrays live in two shared growable pools, `IP` (i32) and `FP` (f64), addressed by offsets stored in each level; a pool reallocates when it grows, which would leave any slice of it dangling, so a pool is never grown while any slice of it is held: every block that appends to a pool first does all of its appending, then takes slices. Only `am_solve`'s setup phase grows the pools; nothing grows them during the solve phase.

```amni
extern fn am_view_f64(id i32, xs real[:]) = "am_view_f64";
extern fn am_view_i32(id i32, xs i32[:]) = "am_view_i32";
```

Struct `Lv` is one multigrid level, holding offsets into the pools: n (rows), nnz (entries), rp (offset in `IP` of n+1 row starts), ci (offset in `IP` of nnz columns), va (offset in `FP` of nnz values), d and r (offsets in `FP` of n entries each), agg (offset in `IP` of n aggregate numbers, or -1 when the level has none), nc (number of aggregates), rb, eb, r2 and e2 (offsets in `FP` of n entries each, or -1 on level 0), ch (offset in `FP` of n times n Cholesky entries, or -1 when the level has none), y (offset in `FP` of n entries, or -1) and gs (true when the coarsest level is solved by Gauss-Seidel sweeps).

Module state: `IP` and `FP` are the pools; `LV` is the growable array of levels, finest first. `RPI`, `CII`, `VAI`, `BI` and `XI` are the input matrix (i32 row starts, i32 columns, f64 values), right-hand side and initial guess, all filled by JavaScript. `AG` is a scratch i32 growable array for aggregation; `GR`, `GC` (i32) and `GV` (f64) are scratch growable arrays for the Galerkin product; `GP` is a scratch i32 growable array of positions. `PR`, `PZ`, `PP` and `PQ` are the conjugate gradient vectors (f64 growable arrays). Small helpers return pool slices: `ipv(off, len)` is `IP` from off for len entries and `fpv(off, len)` is `FP` from off for len entries. `ip_add(len) int` appends len zeros to `IP` and returns the old length; `fp_add(len) int` does the same for `FP`.

`am_alloc(n i32, nnz i32)` sizes `RPI` to n+1 zeros, `CII` to nnz zeros, `VAI` to nnz zeros, `BI` and `XI` to n zeros each, then reports them: `am_view_i32(1, RPI)`, `am_view_i32(2, CII)`, `am_view_f64(3, VAI)`, `am_view_f64(4, BI)` and `am_view_f64(5, XI)`.

`diag_of(l)` fills level l's d, exactly as mold_amg.js `diagOf`: d[i] starts at 0.0 and, for each entry k of row i in order whose column is i, gets va[k] added.

`aggregate(l, th real) int` fills `AG` with level l's aggregate numbers and returns their count nc, exactly as mold_amg.js `aggregate`: `AG` becomes n entries of -1 and nc starts at 0. Entry k of row i is strong when its column j is not i and the absolute value of va[k] is at least th times the square root of the absolute value of d[i] times d[j]. First pass, for each row i in order whose `AG` entry is below 0: ok starts true and has starts false; for each entry k of row i in order that is strong, has becomes true, and when `AG` at its column is at least 0, ok becomes false and the scan stops. When ok and has are both true, `AG`[i] becomes nc, every strong entry k of row i sets `AG` at its column to nc, and nc grows by one. Second pass, for each row i in order whose `AG` entry is still below 0: b starts at -1 and bv at 0.0; for each entry k of row i in order whose column j is not i, whose `AG`[j] is at least 0 and whose absolute value of va[k] is above bv, bv becomes that absolute value and b becomes `AG`[j]. Then `AG`[i] becomes b when b is at least 0, otherwise nc, and nc grows by one in that case.

`galerkin(l, nc)` builds the coarse matrix of level l by summing rows and columns within aggregates, exactly as mold_amg.js `galerkin`, into `GR` (nc+1 row starts), `GC` (columns) and `GV` (values), reading the aggregate numbers from level l's agg. cnt is nc+1 zeros; each row i adds one to cnt[agg[i]+1]; then cnt is turned into running sums (cnt[I+1] gets cnt[I] added, for I in order). mem is n entries and f is a copy of the first nc entries of cnt; for each row i in order, mem[f[agg[i]]] becomes i and that f entry grows by one. `GP` is nc entries of -1, `GR` starts as the single entry 0, and `GC` and `GV` start empty. For each aggregate I in order: s0 is the length of `GC`; for each q from cnt[I] below cnt[I+1], with i equal to mem[q], for each entry k of row i in order, J is agg at column ci[k]: when `GP`[J] is below 0, `GP`[J] becomes the length of `GC`, then J is appended to `GC` and va[k] to `GV`; otherwise `GV`[`GP`[J]] gets va[k] added. Then for q from s0 below the length of `GC`, `GP` at `GC`[q] goes back to -1, and the length of `GC` is appended to `GR`.

`chol(l)` builds the dense Cholesky factor of the coarsest level l into a new block of n times n entries of `FP` (appended first with `fp_add`, its offset stored as the level's ch; the level's y gets `fp_add(n)` too), exactly as mold_amg.js `chol`, with M being that block: mx starts at 0.0; for each row i and each entry k of it in order, M[i·n+ci[k]] gets va[k] added, and when ci[k] is i, mx becomes the larger of mx and the absolute value of va[k]. Then each M[i·n+i] gets 1e-12 times mx plus 1e-300 added. Then for each column j in order: s is M[j·n+j]; for k below j, s gets M[j·n+k] times M[j·n+k] subtracted; s becomes the square root of s when s is above 0, otherwise the square root of 1e-12 times mx plus 1e-300; M[j·n+j] becomes s; and for each i from j+1 below n, t is M[i·n+j], for k below j t gets M[i·n+k] times M[j·n+k] subtracted, and M[i·n+j] becomes t divided by s.

`setup(coarse int, theta real)` builds the levels from the input arrays, exactly as mold_amg.js `amgSetup`: `IP`, `FP` and `LV` are emptied. Level 0 copies `RPI` (n+1 entries), `CII` and `VAI` (nnz entries, where nnz is `RPI`[n]) into newly appended pool space; every level's d and r are appended zeros, its agg and ch are -1, and it is not gs; rb, eb, r2 and e2 are -1 on level 0 and appended zeros on every other level. Then for lev from 0 below 25: `diag_of` of the last level; stop when its n is at most coarse; nc is `aggregate(last, theta)`, and when nc (as a real) is above 0.5 times n, nc is `aggregate(last, 0.0)` instead; stop when nc is above 0.85 times n. Otherwise the last level's agg becomes a copy of `AG` appended to `IP` and its nc becomes nc; `galerkin(last, nc)` runs; and a new level is appended whose n is nc and whose rp, ci and va are copies of `GR`, `GC` and `GV` appended to the pools. When the loop ends after 25 rounds without stopping, the matrix built in the last round is dropped: the levels appended earlier stay and the new one is not appended (append a level only at the start of the next round, right before its `diag_of`). Finally, when the last level's n is at most 1500, `chol` of it runs; otherwise its gs becomes true.

`gs(l, b, x, back)` (b and x are f64 slices, back is a bool) is one Gauss-Seidel sweep on level l, exactly as mold_amg.js `gs`: for s from 0 below n, i is n-1-s when back is true and otherwise s; t starts at b[i] and, for each entry k of row i in order whose column j is not i, gets va[k] times x[j] subtracted; x[i] becomes t divided by d[i] when d[i] is not 0, otherwise 0.

`cyc(l, b, x, w)` (b and x are f64 slices, w is a bool) is one multigrid cycle for level l, exactly as mold_amg.js `cyc`. When the level is gs: x is set to zeros, then 10 times `gs(l, b, x, false)` followed by `gs(l, b, x, true)`, and return. When the level has ch, with M its factor and y its y: forward, for each i in order, t starts at b[i], for k below i gets M[i·n+k] times y[k] subtracted, and y[i] becomes t divided by M[i·n+i]; backward, for i from n-1 down to 0, t starts at y[i], for k from i+1 below n gets M[k·n+i] times x[k] subtracted, and x[i] becomes t divided by M[i·n+i]; then return. Otherwise, with level l+1 as the next level and its rb, eb, r2 and e2 as rc, ec, r2 and e2: x is set to zeros; `gs(l, b, x, false)`; for each row i, t starts at b[i] and gets va[k] times x[ci[k]] subtracted for each entry k in order, and level l's r[i] becomes t; rc is set to zeros, then for each row i in order, rc at agg[i] gets r[i] added; `cyc(l+1, rc, ec, w)`. When w is true and level l+1 is neither gs nor has ch: for each row i of level l+1, t starts at rc[i] and gets level l+1's va[k] times ec at its ci[k] subtracted for each entry k in order, and r2[i] becomes t; `cyc(l+1, r2, e2, w)`; then each ec[i] gets e2[i] added. Finally each x[i] gets ec at agg[i] added, and `gs(l, b, x, true)`.

`mv(v, o)` (f64 slices) multiplies the input matrix (`RPI`, `CII`, `VAI`) by v: for each row i, t starts at 0.0, adds `VAI`[k] times v at `CII`[k] for each entry k in order, and o[i] becomes t.

`am_solve(tol real, maxit i32, coarse i32, theta real, w i32) i32` solves the input matrix times x equals `BI` into `XI`, exactly as mold_amg.js `amgPcg`, and returns the iteration count. n is the length of `BI`. `PR`, `PZ`, `PP` and `PQ` become n zeros each. `mv(XI, PQ)`; bn starts at 0.0; for each i, `PR`[i] becomes `BI`[i] minus `PQ`[i] and bn gets `BI`[i] times `BI`[i] added; bn becomes its square root. When bn is not above 0, `XI` is set to zeros and it returns 0. Otherwise it runs `setup(coarse, theta)`, then `cyc(0, PR, PZ, w is not zero)`, copies `PZ` into `PP`, and rz is the sum of `PR`[i] times `PZ`[i]. it starts at 0; while it is below maxit: rn is the sum of `PR`[i] squared; stop when the square root of rn is at most tol times bn; `mv(PP, PQ)`; pq is the sum of `PP`[i] times `PQ`[i]; a is rz divided by pq; for each i, `XI`[i] gets a times `PP`[i] added and `PR`[i] gets a times `PQ`[i] subtracted; `cyc(0, PR, PZ, w is not zero)`; r2 is the sum of `PR`[i] times `PZ`[i]; be is r2 divided by rz; rz becomes r2; each `PP`[i] becomes `PZ`[i] plus be times `PP`[i]; it grows by one. It returns it.
